    30	1. **Record fields when an article retells an earlier announcement**: role-v53 adopted (D247) ([loop log](research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/loop-log.md) rows 26–30, [folder](research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/fields-2026-10-30/)). Mechanism (five probes): the prompt's one clause "for a retelling, the original speaker and statement day" was read as "a statement the item explicitly attributes", so an action the item narrates got the outlet as speaker and the report day as its day, while the true day went to `event_time`, which no step reads. role-v52 (`poc-v68`) wrote the three fields' definitions into the role text: fields fixed, but about 7 to 15 more gold-rejected statements kept per draw (two runs). role-v53 (`poc-v69`) keeps role-v51's text and carries the same definitions as schema field descriptions (`extraction.FIELD_NOTES`, from version 53): in the simultaneous run U, outlet-as-speaker and report-day signals over 148 articles × 3 draws 166 → 35, verified field errors on a fresh blind read of 24 articles 18/96 → 11/94 records, gold-rejected kept −6.7 per draw, core and supporting within noise (the two largest core "losses" are the same matter quoted by another sentence). Left over: a column restating two companies' reported figures still credited to its author; unnamed executives tiered as third-party views (the tier table says rumour); days weaker than role-v52 (13 against 3 signals).
    31	2. **Summary lines** lose a condition or say more than the quote in 11 of 102 (the Groq "acquisition" in quotation marks written as an acquisition; a deal's "backstop" link left in a neighbouring record); one event is split into several records in 9 (one Reuters rumour as three records).
    32	3. **Noise:** readers call 42 of 102 records noise and Claude agrees with all 42 (a defendant's assets on the optional Singapore case, restated year-old annual figures, a partner's own energy deal, a game list); gold-rejected sentences kept about 100 per draw on the development set.
    33	4. **Questions**, read from the candidates themselves: DRAM contract prices (development) and the $12.9B Hugging Face acquisition (test) are opened in no draw, though the Hugging Face statement is kept in every draw; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is never its own yes/no question, only covered by the wider lease-guarantee amount question; data-centre revenue for FY2028 is asked as total revenue growth (2 of 3 draws); per page the opening varies (the credit-support question on one page in 2 of 3 draws, carried by other pages in every draw). Must-not: the lidar-legislation policy question in 1 of 3 draws (role-v42: 11 of 15); one Nigeria production question in one draw.
