    46	Earlier open defects, unchanged:
    47	
    48	- Shared by both matching engines (log row 49): the same figure stated in another document is often judged new (2–3 of 30 sampled new decisions); one matter can end up in several groups (the capex matter holds two groups in the Jev arm, three in the control); a decision naming a candidate of the same answer breaks a chain or duplicates a thesis (7 decisions over all ledgers, all generative); an empty `original_who` on both sides reads as the same party in the writer's rule; two names for the same population are sometimes kept apart (2 merge misses of 9 with Jev).
    49	- Aggregate theses multiply under the `ai-capex` theme: the Jev arm holds nine 2026 aggregate-spending theses, each a different population under the gold's rule. Whether such variants deserve separate theses is the analyst's judgement, not an identity defect; it is recorded for a product decision.
    50	- The classification simulation keeps the limits in [the preserved defect list](research/2026-10-25-english/classification-existing-defects.txt); a passing simulation is not classification acceptance.
    51	- Corpus: 97 official notices with 56 body and filename collisions need the recorded re-fetch before a rebuilt corpus is relied on.
    52	- The older intermittent search-test failure is untraced; the Docker external-connectivity restriction needs a current check before a container model run.
    53	- Parallel day: the spend cap is checked per committed item, so in-flight early requests can pass it by at most the worker count's calls; the cap counts archived calls, not a retried attempt whose reply was lost (as for the OpenAI client). Re-prepared requests cost about 24% more per day.
