    79	The handoff plan with every open task, the principles and the quantitative criteria is [`handoff-plan-2026-10-30.md`](research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/handoff-plan-2026-10-30.md).
    80	
    81	**Evidence bloat, before anything else downstream:** not solved, reduced at the source. The window rule (D198, judging-v5) hides old pieces by recency, not by count: in the sealed three-month ledger the largest judging request after it (Data Center revenue) still showed 647 pieces (1,185 hidden), 93,634 input tokens, because a broad thesis took 40–60 new links a day. Placement under the one-home rule (D207–D209) cut the first week's links per item 3.3 → 1.82 and the largest thesis's links 106 → 52, the largest judging request 15,229 → 8,649 tokens (`english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28` against the sealed ledger's same week), measured over one week with role-v22 only. The decisive test is the whole-pipeline run over the three-month window with `poc-v69` (on Alex's go, with a cap), read for links per thesis, the largest judging request and any halt; the structural answer, a versioned argument record per question for the judge to read, is designed nowhere yet (handoff plan T6).
    82	
    83	## Parallel day (D172–D183)
    84	
    85	The replay runs the parallel day by default (D183: Alex chose it to converge faster); `scripts/replay.py --workers 1` runs the serial path, needed to resume a day a serial run left unfinished. Measured live on the complete July 27 day (53 items), three serial against three parallel trials interleaved ([result](../poc/reports/2026-10-26-e2e-parallel/results.md)): median 501.8 s → 112.5 s, **4.46× end to end**, 5.91× for item work; daily judging (~37–40 s) is the same code in both modes. Every trial's audit is clean; each parallel trial's runner accepted exactly the rebuilt exact-prefix request for all 53 items; each trial's answers replayed through the other mode give an identical ledger. Drift from the recorded sequential golden is inside the serial trials' own spread. The product entry point (`--workers 16`) ran the same day live in 135.5 s with the same checks clean. On recorded answers the parallel day equals the golden in all ten tables (D180, and again on the promoted module).
    86	
    87	Fifteen consecutive days (August 1–15, 221 items) with every call answered by one deterministic oracle of the request bytes, at no cost (D182, [result](../poc/reports/2026-10-26-multi-day-oracle/results.md)): the parallel ledger equals the serial one in all 20 product tables, its turns accepted exactly the serial run's 221 extraction requests, and a broken runner was caught. At recorded call durations the fifteen days take an estimated 2.79× less time (2,512 s → 900 s); a busy day gains 3.6–4.5×, a day of a few items 1.5–2×.
    88	
    89	What remains of the time is not scheduling: items that depend on the item just before them (~45–60 s), daily judging (D63), and each item's two matching phases in order (~17 s). Ten times end to end on this day would need those to shrink.

